      {
        "ballot_id": "B035",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B011",
    "B003"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B011 to B035. The discussion clarifies that optical lattice clocks join a sharply defined physical control principle—magic-wavelength confinement—with experimental realization whose systematic performance has been independently reproduced. This is a mature capability for precision comparisons and tests involving gravity and fundamental physics, not simply improved timekeeping. Katori and Ye remain a compact, intelligible pairing of the decisive concept and defining experimental establishment, provided the citation is confined to lattice clocks rather than the whole optical-clock field.",
    "case_against": "The remaining concern is that an instrument prize can blur the boundary between the original lattice-clock principle, later systematic-control advances, and future applications. The exact historical division of credit among other optical-clock pioneers still requires scrutiny. B011 remains exceptionally strong in durability and technological consequence, but the supplied discussion leaves its division between original compositions, processing, and later optimization less resolved than is suitable for a final public configuration.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that magic-wavelength lattice clocks achieved extraordinary, independently reproducible stability and accuracy, with Katori and Ye representing the central conceptual and experimental advances.",
        "response": "I find this decisive on reassessment. The reproducibility and direct control of the key systematic limitation give B035 the robust physics-to-measurement connection that I require. The citation should retain Kroll's narrow boundary and make no claim to the entire precision-metrology enterprise."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson supports B011 as a durable materials invention but requires archival separation of Sagawa's and Croat's original work from later composition, processing, and industrial development.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B011 as a major materials achievement and a serious alternative. However, that unresolved archival separation bears directly on the exact two-person attribution. With no further primary historical evidence in this discussion, I give priority to B035, whose physical principle and proposed credit line are presently more narrowly stated."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that fluctuation theorems are universal, experimentally tested relations, while recognizing questions about priority among formulations and the absence of one defining experimental discovery.",
        "response": "The conceptual unity of B027 is impressive, and its tests matter. I nevertheless favor B035 because its central physical mechanism, experimental realization, and reproducible performance form a more singular and directly delimited achievement, while B027 still requires finer adjudication among related formulations."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review fully supports limiting the central credit for optical lattice clocks to Katori and Ye rather than including another indispensable optical-clock pioneer.",
      "How narrowly the public citation can isolate magic-wavelength lattice clocks from later systematic-control work and broader applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile; I have no disclosed collaboration or institutional connection requiring recusal from the proposed laureates."
  }
}
